, El Pollo Loco, Inc., develops, franchises, licenses, and operates quick-service restaurants under the El Pollo Loco name. The company operates and franchises restaurants located in California, Nevada, Arizona, Texas, Colorado, Utah, Louisiana, New Mexico, and Washington. It also licenses its brand to restaurants in the Philippines. The company was formerly known as Chicken Acquisition Corp. and changed its name to El Pollo Loco Holdings, Inc. in April 2014. El Pollo Loco Holdings, Inc. was founded in 1975 and is headquartered in Costa Mesa, California.","ceo_bio":"Ms. Elizabeth Goodwin Williams | CEO & Director | Age 49 | Compensation: $1.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 4K people. Revenue per employee of $123K ranks in the bottom quartile among Consumer Cyclical peers. Each employee generates $6K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $1K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 11\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["El Pollo Loco Holdings, Inc. is a small-cap consumer cyclical business with a $454M market cap on $490M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +1% over the past three years and +3% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 9% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically.","The business throws off $25M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 5 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.82\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.8\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage runs at 9.4\u00d7.","Shareholder yield is only 0.2%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 2.5% of the 